  "narrative": "I assessed all newly delivered results with the returned estimates already signed to each claim's registered direction. Positive signed estimates support the registered direction, negative signed estimates support the opposite direction, and intervals crossing zero leave the direction uncertain. The main practical question is whether these follow-up results modify or threaten the accepted large associations H8, H9, H12, and H13, or reveal artifact/technical confounding.\n\nThe batch-robustness interactions for WRN and USP7 do not establish meaningful heterogeneity. H27 (MSI-high WRN effect in batch_a) has signed estimate +0.0476 (95% CI -0.0195 to +0.1146), suggesting possibly slightly stronger effect in batch_a but imprecise; relative to the accepted main effect of +0.790, this is small, so H27 is unresolved/deferred rather than a meaningful modifier. H23 (TP53-intact USP7 effect in batch_a) has +0.0290 (CI -0.1282 to +0.1862); it is imprecise and does not support a clear batch modifier claim, so unresolved/deferred. These interactions do not overturn H8 or H12.\n\nThe SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 batch interaction H28 is more informative but still small. Its signed estimate is -0.0509 (CI -0.0950 to -0.0069). Because H28 was signed such that positive evidence would indicate a stronger accepted effect in batch_a, this negative signed result indicates the opposite: the SMARCA4-loss association is modestly weaker in batch_a by roughly 0.05 on the signed scale. This is statistically nonzero at 95%, but the magnitude is tiny relative to the accepted main SMARCA4-loss effect of +0.784 and should be interpreted cautiously given multiple testing. I therefore reject the stronger-in-batch claim registered as H28 and close it, while keeping H9 accepted. The small attenuation is a reason to request independent validation of H9: independent evidence would help confirm that the main effect is not batch-specific or attenuated in an unstable way.\n\nThe EGFR follow-up in KRAS-wild-type lines, H14, gives +0.0162 (CI -0.0135 to +0.0459) for dependency_EGFR in EGFR-amplified versus non-amplified cells. This is in the expected direction but imprecise and modest; it is not enough to accept or reject a meaningful EGFR-dependency association. H14 remains unresolved/active, and the original full-population H5 also remains unresolved.\n\nThe ARID1A-loss modifier interaction for SMARCA4 loss on SMARCA2, H20, is +0.0373 (CI -0.0148 to +0.0894). It is small relative to the main SMARCA4 effect and statistically inconclusive. There is no evidence that ARID1A loss strongly changes the accepted SMARCA4-SMARCA2 association, but the interval does not exclude small modification; H20 is unresolved/deferred.\n\nThe negative-control tests are largely reassuring against simple constructed-marker artifacts. H18 (research marker E with SMARCA2), H22 (marker F with NFE2L2), and H35 (marker F with WRN) are all very small and statistically inconclusive; H18 and H35 estimates are actually opposite their registered directions, and H22 is opposite. Given the large sizes of the accepted biological effects, these marker nulls do not support a marker-driven artifact explanation. I reject these marker-association claims and close them. H36 (marker D with USP7) has a small +0.0123 (CI -0.0225 to +0.0470), so it is not supported, but its interval is wide enough that I keep it unresolved/deferred rather than declare a fully excluded null; it remains far smaller than the accepted USP7 TP53 effect of +0.396 and does not currently threaten H12.\n\nThe new mechanistic discovery tests were mostly negative. MYC amplification against RPL11 dependency, H29, has -0.0125 (CI -0.0339 to +0.0090), opposite the registered expected direction and not statistically supported; the possible positive side is small, so I reject it. EGFR amplification against STAT3 dependency, H33, has -0.0336 (CI -0.0681 to +0.0009), also opposite the registered direction and with only a trivial possible effect in the claimed direction; I reject it. BRCA2 loss against WRN dependency, H31, is essentially null (-0.0001; CI -0.0449 to +0.0448). This is not supportive, but the interval is wide and could include a modest effect, so H31 is left unresolved/deferred rather than closed.\n\nOverall, the new results do not undermine the four main accepted associations. They do, however, highlight a small batch-directional attenuation for H9 and leave some minor modifier questions unresolved. I request independent validation for H9 because it is a central accepted association with a large main effect, no prior independent validation, and a new modest batch-interaction signal that makes independent confirmation useful.",
  "research_notes": "Main accepted dataset-supported associations after this appraise: H8 MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency; H9 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency (validation requested because of small batch attenuation); H12 TP53 intact -> stronger USP7 dependency; H13 KEAP1 loss -> less-negative NFE2L2 dependency. Rejected/closed: H2, H6, H7, H10, H11, plus new negative/control/discovery claims H18, H22, H28 as stronger-in-batch, H29, H33, H35. Unresolved active: H1, H3, H4, H5, H14. Unresolved deferred modifiers/controls: H20 ARID1A modification, H23 USP7 batch modification, H27 WRN batch modification, H31 BRCA2-WRN, H36 marker-D USP7. Next priorities: assess H9 independent validation response; then likely validate H8 or H12 if useful; continue pending lineage/other modifiers H15, H16, H17, H19, H21, H24, H25, H26, H30, H32, H34; interpret all as associations in supplied dataset only.",
