er SMARCA2 dependency, including in ARID1A-loss and ARID1A-intact contexts; TP53-intact status is associated with stronger USP7 dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker or less-negative NFE2L2 dependency; and PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency across major tested contexts. These remain associations in the supplied dataset, not causal claims or claims of generalizability to other cohorts.\n\nNegative and null results continue to delimit the biological scope of these associations. The robust SMARCA2 dependency signal is tied to SMARCA4 loss rather than ARID1A loss alone. PTEN/STAT3 lineage and PIK3CA interaction checks were null, and the many pathway co-mutation probes tested so far are generally null or small. EGFR-amplification effects on TMED10 and ERBB2 were not supported, and constructed marker controls behaved as expected as null references. Unresolved uncertainty is concentrated in imprecise or borderline smaller effects: EGFR-amplification main and clean-context checks, KRAS/BRAF pathway contrasts, USP7 PIK3CA modification, and the pending PTEN/STAT3 KRAS and SMAD4 interaction checks. The most informative next analyses are already registered: H97 and H98 for PTEN/STAT3 modification by KRAS and SMAD4, H73 for USP7/TP53 modification by PIK3CA, the EGFR amplification context set, and remaining active unresolved predictors. I am not requesting validation for these pending modifiers because none has yet shown a large robust discovery signal; validation capacity should be reserved if a new large effect emerges.",
