  "narrative": "The response deadline concerns H124, the association between CDKN2A loss and dependency_MDM2. I retain the accepted claim. Both discovery and independent validation are direction-consistent: with direction -1, positive signed values indicate that CDKN2A-loss cells have more negative, stronger MDM2 dependency than CDKN2A-intact cells. Discovery gives +0.117 (95% CI +0.082 to +0.152; R130), and independent validation gives +0.132 (adjusted interval +0.080 to +0.184; R143). The validation lower bound is slightly below my practical benchmark of roughly 0.1 dependency-score units, but the central estimates exceed the benchmark, the signs replicate, and stratum analyses are consistent. I therefore accept H124 as a scientifically meaningful dataset association with the caveat that its practical magnitude is borderline at the lower confidence limit and does not establish mechanism.\n\nFinal scientific report. The supplied 2000-cell lung-cancer CRISPR screen dataset supports a compact set of genotype/context associations with dependency scores. Dependency scores are continuous and more negative values indicate stronger dependency; all reported signed estimates below are already oriented to the claim direction. A practical benchmark of approximately 0.1 dependency-score-unit difference was used because a percentage-based clinical threshold does not translate naturally to signed continuous dependency scores.\n\nSupported primary associations. First, MSI-high cells show much stronger dependency_WRN than MSS cells. Discovery gives +0.790 (95% CI +0.760 to +0.819; R8), and independent validation gives +0.771 (adjusted interval +0.720 to +0.822; R39). The effect persists across tested strata: adenocarcinoma, batch_a, Avana library, KY library, TP53-intact cells, and high-mutation-burden contexts (H39, H45, H62, H109, H122, H150). Interaction tests in lineage, batch, aneuploidy, and mutation-burden contexts did not support meaningful modification, though the high-mutation-burden analysis must be interpreted cautiously because MSI-high status and mutation burden are correlated. The conclusion is dataset-supported but not causal: MSI-high cells are markedly dependent on WRN in this screen.\n\nSecond, SMARCA4 loss is strongly associated with stronger dependency_SMARCA2. Discovery gives +0.784 (95% CI +0.765 to +0.803; R9), and validation gives +0.807 (adjusted interval +0.777 to +0.838; R26). The association is robust in arid1a_loss=0 and arid1a_loss=1 subgroups, adenocarcinoma, batch_a, KY library, and TP53-loss cells (H37, H38, H46, H49, H63, H116). ARID1A loss has no meaningful additional effect within SMARCA4-loss cells, and interactions with lineage, TP53 loss, growth rate, stemness, ploidy, or batch are not supported. This supports a strong dataset association, compatible with paralog or SWI/SNF context dependency but not proof of mechanism.\n\nThird, TP53-intact cells show stronger dependency_USP7 than TP53-loss cells. The TP53-intact-minus-TP53-loss contrast gives +0.396 discovery (95% CI +0.325 to +0.467; R12) and +0.435 validation (adjusted interval +0.334 to +0.536; R40). It is also seen in KRAS-mutant, KRAS-wild-type, adenocarcinoma, batch_a, KY library, and STK11-loss strata (H40, H41, H47, H51, H64, H115). Interaction tests by KRAS mutation, batch, lineage, aneuploidy, PTEN loss, or other modifiers are null. The evidence supports a robust dataset association between TP53 loss and weaker USP7 dependency, or TP53 integrity and stronger USP7 dependency.\n\nFourth, KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker, less negative dependency_NFE2L2 scores rather than stronger dependency. With direction reversed from the initial hypothesis, the signed discovery estimate is +0.796 (95% CI +0.776 to +0.816; R7) and validation is +0.781 (adjusted interval +0.752 to +0.809; R13). The association is consistent in TP53-intact and TP53-loss cells, batch_a, adenocarcinoma, KY library, STK11-loss cells, and other strata (H42, H43, H48, H50, H65, H114). Interaction tests by TP53 loss, lineage, MYC RNA, PIK3CA mutation, or other modifiers are null. Thus, in this dataset KEAP1 loss marks cells with weaker apparent NFE2L2 dependency.\n\nFifth, PTEN loss is strongly associated with stronger dependency_STAT3. Discovery gives +0.267 (95% CI +0.215 to +0.319; R73), and independent validation gives +0.274 (adjusted interval +0.200 to +0.348; R79). This is one of the most reproducible findings. It is observed in the main population and in adenocarcinoma, TP53-intact, TP53-loss, KY-library, Avana-library, STK11-loss, batch_a, adherent-growth, high-IFN-gamma, and high-hypoxia strata (H85, H87, H90, H91, H105, H112, H113, H111, H123, H131, H132). The main-population interaction tests show amplification of the PTEN-loss effect in high IFN-gamma contexts: H134 gives +0.221 (95% CI +0.104 to +0.338; R144), and the stricter IFN>=0.66 threshold gives +0.250 (95% CI +0.129 to +0.370; R157). H146 at IFN>=0 is positive but its lower bound is near zero, so it is not accepted. The IFN-gamma result should be treated as dataset-supported context dependence, not general causal evidence.\n\nSixth, CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger dependency_MDM2. The main finding is accepted above. Stratum consistency includes TP53-intact, TP53-loss, and batch_a contexts (H125, H126, H128). Interaction tests by TP53 loss, MYC amplification, MTAP loss, growth rate, or other modifiers do not show meaningful modification. This is a scientifically meaningful association, but it is the smallest primary accepted effect and has lower-bound uncertainty near the practical threshold.\n\nImportant negative findings. Canonical gene-to-dependency expectations are often null in this dataset: KRAS mutation does not show a meaningful association with dependency_KRAS; BRAF V600E does not show a meaningful association with dependency_BRAF or dependency_KRAS; EGFR amplification does not show a meaningful association with dependency_EGFR; ERBB2 amplification does not show a meaningful association with dependency_ERBB2. MYC amplification is null across the many outcomes tested, including dependency_RPL11, POLR2A, DHX9, KIF18A, TMED10, MDM2, and SMARCA2. PTEN loss is not associated with dependency_USP7, dependency_MDM2, or dependency_EGFR in tested settings. TP53 loss alone is not meaningfully associated with dependency_MDM2, dependency_STAT3, or dependency_WRN after direct testing. The constructed research markers D, E, and F are generally null, supporting that they are not driving the accepted gene-context findings. These negative results are informative because they indicate that the large accepted associations are not simply generic effects of copy-number burden, proliferation, arbitrary marker assays, or the most obvious gene-by-gene mapping.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty. The PTEN-loss/dependency_STAT3 association is robust, but its EMT context dependence is unresolved. H143 is borderline (+0.141, 95% CI +0.011 to +0.271; R153), H148 is null, and H149 is borderline; H158, a direct PTEN-loss mean difference within EMT>=0.66, remains pending. Lower-threshold IFN sensitivity H146 is weak and unresolved. H118, PTEN/STAT3 modification by TP53 loss, remains uncertain because the interval crosses zero. H98 and H138 are analytic specification failures due insufficient interaction cells, not evidence of absence. Independent validation capacity is exhausted, so all remaining refinements are discovery-stage dataset associations and remain subject to multiplicity and threshold-selection concerns. No claim establishes causality, therapeutic vulnerability, or external generalizability to other CRISPR platforms, cohorts, or biological contexts.\n\nNext analysis. The most informative next tests are to complete the pending EMT sensitivity work and to add a direct high-IFN-gamma stratum estimate. I registered H159: PTEN-loss versus no-PTEN-loss dependency_STAT3 mean difference in cells with IFN-gamma signature>=0.66. This separates the absolute PTEN-loss effect in the high-IFN stratum from the accepted interaction. Together with H158, the direct EMT-high estimate, this will determine whether the IFN and EMT results reflect robust context-specific biology or threshold-sensitive interaction artifacts. If both direct stratum estimates remain above the practical threshold and agree with the interactions, I would strengthen them as supported context-dependent dataset findings. If they cross zero or have lower bounds far below the benchmark, I would interpret the IFN and EMT modifiers as unstable or insufficiently supported.",
