  "narrative": "No assessments_due were listed and no new validation was released; the independent validation budget is exhausted. I therefore used synthesis to close two stale evidence items. I rejected H118, PTEN-loss modification of dependency_STAT3 by TP53 loss, because the signed estimate was +0.097 with interval -0.024 to +0.217. The point is below my practical dependency benchmark of about 0.1 score units, the interval crosses null, and direct PTEN effects were already accepted in both TP53-intact and TP53-loss strata, so there is no meaningful evidence of modification. I closed H98 as unresolved because one interaction cell had only 19 observations, making it an analytic specification failure rather than evidence for or against modification.\n\nScale and decision rule. The supplied clinical 10 percent rule does not translate cleanly to signed CRISPR dependency scores, which are continuous and mostly negative and where more negative values mean stronger dependency. I therefore judged scientific meaningfulness primarily by absolute dependency-score difference. A difference above about 0.1 score units is substantial on the scale of these outcomes, often roughly one-half SD or more for the canonical dependencies, while 0.0 to 0.1 is treated as small or practically weak. Effects with very large intervals, threshold sensitivity, or lower bounds near zero are called uncertain or rejected for meaningfulness even when point estimates are positive.\n\nSupported primary associations. The strongest validated dataset association is MSI-high versus MSS with dependency_WRN. Discovery gives +0.790, 95 percent interval +0.760 to +0.819, R8, and independent validation gives +0.771, adjusted interval +0.720 to +0.822, R39. The association is more negative, hence stronger, WRN dependency in MSI-high cells and remains in adenocarcinoma, batch_a, Avana library, KY library, TP53-intact cells, and high-mutation-burden strata. Interaction tests by lineage, batch, aneuploidy, and mutation burden did not support meaningful modification, although mutation-burden context must be interpreted cautiously because MSI-high and mutation burden are correlated. This is a dataset-supported marker of WRN dependency, not proof of causality or generalizable therapeutic vulnerability.\n\nSMARCA4 loss is strongly associated with stronger dependency_SMARCA2. Discovery gives +0.784, interval +0.765 to +0.803, R9, and validation gives +0.807, adjusted interval +0.777 to +0.838, R26. It is consistent across ARID1A status, adenocarcinoma, batch_a, KY library, and TP53-loss strata. ARID1A loss has no meaningful additional effect within SMARCA4-loss cells, and interactions by lineage, batch, growth rate, stemness, ploidy, or TP53 loss are unsupported. This is compatible with a paralog or SWI/SNF dependency mechanism, but the current evidence supports association in this screen, not mechanism.\n\nTP53-intact cells show stronger dependency_USP7 than TP53-loss cells. The TP53-intact-minus-loss contrast gives +0.396 discovery, interval +0.325 to +0.467, R12, and +0.435 validation, adjusted interval +0.334 to +0.536, R40. It persists in KRAS-mutant, KRAS-wild-type, adenocarcinoma, batch_a, KY library, and STK11-loss strata. Interaction tests by KRAS mutation, batch, lineage, aneuploidy, PTEN loss, and CDKN2A loss are null. The evidence supports a robust association between TP53 loss and weaker USP7 dependency in this dataset.\n\nKEAP1 loss is associated with weaker, less negative dependency_NFE2L2 scores rather than stronger dependency. This is the reversed-direction accepted association H13: discovery +0.796, interval +0.776 to +0.816, R7, and validation +0.781, adjusted interval +0.752 to +0.809, R13. It is consistent across TP53 status, batch_a, adenocarcinoma, KY library, and STK11-loss strata. Interaction tests by TP53 loss, lineage, MYC RNA, PIK3CA mutation, and other modifiers are null. The interpretation is that KEAP1-loss cells in this screen appear to have weaker