 as a supported effect modifier. TP53-loss modification of PTEN/STAT3 is now rejected. Marker D was used as an arbitrary negative-control interaction for PTEN/STAT3 and was null, which supports specificity against arbitrary marker contexts. The matched RNA+DNA subset also shows the PTEN/STAT3 effect, reducing concern that missing omics alone drives the result.\n\nImportant negative findings. Many intuitive gene-to-dependency expectations are null in this dataset: KRAS mutation is not meaningfully associated with dependency_KRAS; BRAF V600E is not meaningfully associated with dependency_BRAF or dependency_KRAS; EGFR amplification is not meaningfully associated with dependency_EGFR; and ERBB2 amplification is not meaningfully associated with dependency_ERBB2. MYC amplification is null across several outcomes tested, including RPL11, POLR2A, DHX9, KIF18A, TMED10, MDM2, and SMARCA2. PTEN loss is not associated with dependency_USP7, dependency_MDM2, dependency_WRN, or dependency_EGFR in tested main analyses. TP53 loss alone is not meaningfully associated with dependency_MDM2, dependency_STAT3, or dependency_WRN. The constructed research markers and most marker/signature probes are generally null. These negatives are scientifically useful because they argue that the large accepted associations are not simply generic proliferation, copy-number burden, screen-technical artifacts, arbitrary-marker artifacts, or naive gene-by-gene mapping.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty. The remaining uncertainty is mainly about context specificity of PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency, especially EMT, and about whether accepted associations generalize beyond this lung CRISPR screen population. Mutation-burden and SMAD4 interaction tests failed because of insufficient cell counts, not because absence was proven. No analysis establishes causality, therapeutic vulnerability, or generalization to other CRISPR platforms, cell contexts, or patient cohorts.\n\nMost informative next analysis. I registered two direct PTEN-loss versus no-PTEN-loss dependency_STAT3 mean differences in low IFN-gamma and low EMT strata. These are the most informative next discovery tests because they test whether the accepted high-context effects are context-exclusive or whether PTEN loss has broad effects across contexts. For direction -1, a positive signed estimate means PTEN-loss cells have stronger dependency_STAT3. If low-stratum estimates remain positive and above the practical benchmark, the high-IFN and high-EMT findings should be interpreted as amplification on top of a broad PTEN effect. If low-stratum estimates are null or opposite, context specificity becomes stronger. If more resources were available, the next highest-value test would be independent prespecified replication of the IFN-gamma modifier, because it is the only context effect that survived multiple interaction tests.",
