uld distinguish broad effect with high-IFN amplification from a context-exclusive high-IFN effect. H178 rejects the hypothesis that CDKN2A-loss MDM2 dependency is specifically amplified in high MYC-RNA context: signed +0.078 with interval crossing null and point below the practical benchmark. H179 and H180 reject MYC-amplification effects on RPL11 and POLR2A even when MYC RNA is high, consistent with the broader MYC-amplification null pattern. H181 shows only a small signed effect for KIF18A in TP53-intact versus TP53-loss cells (-0.021, -0.040 to -0.001), opposite the registered direction and far below the practical benchmark; statistical exclusion of zero is not enough to call it meaningful. Overall, these results strengthen the PTEN-loss/STAT3 and core dependency associations as broad dataset effects, while keeping low-IFN context specificity unresolved and leaving high-IFN amplification as the clearest supported modifier. Validation requests are exhausted, so all conclusions remain dataset-specific associations rather than causal or externally generalizable claims.",
