  "narrative": "No new validation results were released, and independent validation requests are exhausted. Therefore all conclusions below are associations in this supplied lung CRISPR screen dataset, not causal claims and not claims of external generalizability. I revised one contextual claim: H146, the PTEN-loss interaction for dependency_STAT3 in ifn_gamma_signature >= 0, is changed to reject. Its signed estimate was +0.115, 95% CI +0.012 to +0.219. Although the interval excludes zero, the lower bound is far below my practical dependency-score benchmark of about 0.1 absolute score units, and broad IFN-gamma threshold sensitivity is a concern. Thus the evidence does not support a meaningful amplification of the PTEN-loss STAT3 association at the broad IFN >= 0 threshold. The accepted IFN-gamma amplification claims remain only the higher-threshold interactions H134 and H147. I retained H170 as unresolved and active because direct PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency in the lowest IFN-gamma stratum is positive, +0.138, 95% CI +0.041 to +0.235, but the lower bound is weak and the exposed PTEN-loss cell is small. Pending direct tests H182 and H183 remain the most informative next analyses.\n\nDecision rule. The supplied 10 percent clinical rule does not translate cleanly to CRISPR dependency scores because many scores are negative and are expressed in arbitrary units. I therefore used a conservative practical absolute-score benchmark: differences above about 0.1 score units are substantial, 0 to 0.1 are small or practically weak, and effects with lower bounds near or below 0.1 or threshold sensitivity are treated cautiously. This is much stricter than a literal 10 percent relative change near the typical absolute score magnitude, so accepted effects should be interpreted as robustly meaningful on the scale of these outcomes, not as weak threshold-passing signals.\n\nSupported primary associations. 1. MSI-high versus MSS is strongly associated with stronger dependency_WRN, meaning more negative WRN dependency scores. Discovery +0.790, 95% CI +0.760 to +0.819, R8; independent validation +0.771, adjusted interval +0.720 to +0.822, R39. Consistency is strong across adenocarcinoma, batch_a, Avana and KY libraries, TP53-intact cells, high mutation burden, default RNA+WGS, and other strata. Interaction tests did not support meaningful modification, although mutation-burden interaction testing is limited because all MSI-high cells had high mutation burden. This is a dataset-supported marker association, not proof of causation or therapeutic vulnerability.\n\n2. SMARCA4 loss is strongly associated with stronger dependency_SMARCA2. Discovery +0.784, 95% CI +0.765 to +0.803, R9; independent validation +0.807, adjusted interval +0.777 to +0.838, R26. It is consistent across ARID1A status, adenocarcinoma, batch_a, KY library, TP53-loss status, growth rate, stemness, ploidy, and default RNA+WGS contexts. ARID1A loss adds no meaningful effect within SMARCA4-loss cells. This is compatible with SWI/SNF or paralog vulnerability biology, but the current evidence supports association only.\n\n3. TP53-intact cells show stronger dependency_USP7 than TP53-loss cells. The TP53-intact-minus-loss contrast is +0.396 discovery, 95% CI +0.325 to +0.467, R12; independent validation +0.435, adjusted interval +0.334 to +0.536, R40. The association persists in KRAS-mutant, KRAS-wild-type, adenocarcinoma, batch_a, KY-library, STK11-loss, and other strata. Interaction tests by KRAS mutation, batch, lineage, aneuploidy, PTEN loss, CDKN2A loss, and other modifiers are unsupported.\n\n4. KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker apparent dependency_NFE2L2, i.e. less negative NFE2L2 scores, rather than stronger NFE2L2 dependency. The accepted reversed-direction contrast is +0.796 discovery, 95% CI +0.776 to +0.816, R7; independent validation +0.781, adjusted interval +0.752 to +0.809, R13. It is consistent across TP53 status, batch_a, adenocarcinoma, KY-library, STK11-loss, and default RNA+WGS contexts. Modifier interactions are unsupported.\n\n5. PTEN loss is robustly associated with stronger dependency_STAT3. Discovery +0.267, 95% CI +0.215 to +0.319, R73; independent validation +0.274, adjusted interval +0.200 to +0.348, R79. The main effect has the strongest external validation among the newly accepted associations and is also stable across adenocarcinoma, TP53-intact and TP53-loss, Avana and KY libraries, STK11-loss, batch_a, adherent growth, high hypoxia, high IFN-gamma, high EMT, matched RNA/DNA or RNA+WGS profiles, PIK3CA-mutant cells, and low EMT. The strongest context evidence is amplification in high IFN-gamma: H134 interaction at IFN >= 0.5 gives +0.221, 95% CI +0.104 to +0.338, and H147 at IFN >= 0.66 gives +0.250, 95% CI +0.129 to +0.370. EMT direct PTEN-loss effects are present in low and high EMT strata, but EMT interaction tests are not supported because lower bounds are weak or intervals cross zero and results are threshold-sensitive. Thus EMT should be described as a context where PTEN-loss effects occur, not as a supported modifier. TP53-loss modification of PTEN/STAT3 is unsupported. Marker D interaction is null, supporting specificity against arbitrary marker contexts. The PTEN/STAT3 main effect is validated; the IFN-gamma interaction is not independently validated and remains threshold-dependent.\n\n6. CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger dependency_MDM2. Discovery +0.117, 95% CI +0.082 to +0.152, R130; independent validation +0.132, adjusted interval +0.080 to +0.184, R143. This is the smallest accepted primary association, but the direction and magnitude are consistent and the main effect validates. Interactions by TP53 loss, MYC amplification, MTAP loss, growth rate, and other tested modifiers are unsupported. High MYC-RNA context does not add evidence that CDKN2A-loss MDM2 dependency is stronger.\n\nImportant negative findings. The accepted associations are not explained by naive gene-to-dependency mapping or generic proliferation, copy-number, omics-missingness, arbitrary-marker, or screen-technical artifacts. KRAS mutation is not meaningfully associated with dependency_KRAS; BRAF V600E is not meaningfully associated with dependency_BRAF or dependency_KRAS; EGFR amplification is not meaningfully associated with dependency_EGFR; and ERBB2 amplification is not meaningfully associated with dependency_ERBB2. MYC amplification is null across many outcomes tested, including RPL11, POLR2A, DHX9, KIF18A, TMED10, MDM2, and SMARCA2, including in high-MYC-RNA contexts. PTEN loss is not associated with dependency_USP7, dependency_MDM2, dependency_WRN, or dependency_EGFR in tested main analyses. TP53 loss alone is not meaningfully associated with dependency_MDM2, dependency_STAT3, or dependency_WRN. Constructed research markers and most signature probes are generally null. These negatives strengthen specificity of the accepted associations but do not prove mechanism.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty. The main unresolved biological uncertainty is whether PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency is broadly present and merely amplified in high IFN-gamma, or whether it is strongly context-specific. H170 gives low-IFN direct evidence in the expected direction but with a lower confidence bound below the practical benchmark and a small exposed cell. Pending H182 and H183, which directly test PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency in IFN <= 0 and IFN <= 0 plus EMT <= 0, are the most informative next discovery analyses. I also registered orthogonal direct tests for low IFN with high EMT and high IFN with low EMT to separate IFN and EMT context. The accepted high-IFN amplification remains unvalidated at the interaction level. Mutation-burden and SMAD4 interaction analyses failed because of insufficient cell counts, not because absence was proven. No analysis establishes causality, therapeutic efficacy, or generalization beyond this dataset and CRISPR platform.\n\nMost informative next analysis. Analyze H182 and H183 first. For direction -1 mean differences, a positive signed estimate means PTEN-loss cells have stronger dependency_STAT3. If both low-IFN direct estimates remain positive with lower bounds near or above about 0.1, the best interpretation is a broad PTEN-loss STAT3 association with additional high-IFN amplification. If they are null, opposite, or unstable, the accepted high-IFN interactions should be interpreted as evidence of context specificity. If more capacity remains, the orthogonal low-IFN/high-EMT and high-IFN/low-EMT tests would help determine whether IFN and EMT modify the PTEN-loss effect independently or jointly.",
  "research_notes": "ITERATION 15 SYNTHESIS. Validation budget exhausted; no new validation. Final conclusions are dataset associations. Reassessed H146 from accept to reject: PTEN-loss STAT3 interaction in ifn_gamma >= 0 has signed +0.115, CI +0.012 to +0.219; lower bound below practical 0.1 benchmark and threshold-sensitive. High IFN amplification remains accepted only at IFN >= 0.5 and IFN >= 0.66 interactions. H170 remains unresolved and active: low IFN direct PTEN-loss STAT3 +0.138, CI +0.041 to +0.235, small exposed cell, lower bound below benchmark. H182 and H183 are pending and should be analyzed next. Registered two orthogonal context tests: PTEN-loss STAT3 direct in IFN <= 0 and EMT >= 0.5; and IFN >= 0.5 and EMT <= 0. Accepted core validated associations: MSI-high stronger WRN; SMARCA4 loss stronger SMARCA2; TP53-intact stronger USP7; KEAP1 loss weaker NFE2L2; PTEN loss stronger STAT3; CDKN2A loss stronger MDM2. Negative/unsupported: gene-specific BRAF/EGFR/ERBB2/KRAS mutation expectations; MYC amplification broadly null; arbitrary markers mostly null; most interactions null including EMT as modifier; PTEN main effect not modified by TP53, KRAS, PIK3CA, STK11, lineage, batch, marker D; CDKN2A not modified by TP53/MYC/MTAP/growth; USP7 not modified by KRAS/batch/lineage/aneuploidy/PTEN/CDKN2A; WRN modification unsupported but mutation-burden interaction invalid due zero cell. Scale rule: absolute dependency-score difference >0.1 substantial; lower bounds near/below 0.1 or threshold-sensitive effects unresolved or rejected. Generalizability/causality unknown."
