  "narrative":
    "All estimates are interpreted exactly as signed for the registered hypothesis direction. For the direction -1 PTEN-loss and other exposed-minus-comparator contrasts, a positive value means the exposed group has a more negative dependency score, i.e. stronger dependency. For the direction +1 KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 contrast, a positive value means weaker apparent NFE2L2 dependency in KEAP1-loss cells. I used the established conservative practical benchmark for these CRISPR scores: a substantial association requires a signed estimate above roughly 0.1 absolute dependency-score units with a lower confidence bound not falling far below that threshold; smaller point estimates or lower bounds near zero are treated as weak or unresolved.\n\nThe key new PTEN-loss/STAT3 results directly address whether the accepted PTEN association is broad or context-specific. H182 gives a robust positive effect in the low IFN-gamma stratum, +0.209, 95% CI +0.136 to +0.281, with adequate cell sizes. H183 supports the same effect in cells with low IFN and low EMT, +0.224, CI +0.117 to +0.331. H184 supports it in low IFN with high EMT, +0.302, CI +0.152 to +0.451. H185 supports it in high IFN with low EMT, +0.361, CI +0.228 to +0.493. These strata are small at the exposed side in H183-H185, but the lower bounds are sufficiently above the practical benchmark to accept the direct effects with caution. Collectively they support a broad PTEN-loss association with stronger STAT3 dependency in this dataset, not an effect confined only to high IFN. The high-IFN interaction remains a supported amplification, but it is better interpreted as amplification over a broad baseline effect rather than a context-exclusive signal.\n\nH186-H188 further strengthen the PTEN-loss/STAT3 association by showing it persists in high-Cas9 screens, low mutation burden, and low EMT: +0.263, +0.227, and +0.268 respectively, all with lower bounds above the benchmark. H186 is technically a subset selection on Cas9 activity, so it tests whether the association is inflated by poor-quality screens; it does not provide external validation and cannot exclude selection bias. H187 addresses a high-TMB confounding concern, and H188 further shows that PTEN loss is not merely a high-EMT proxy.\n\nH189-H192 are high-Cas9 robustness checks for core accepted associations. All remain very strong: MSI-high/WRN +0.780, SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 +0.791, TP53-intact/USP7 +0.385, and KEAP1-loss/weak NFE2L2 dependency +0.798. These lower bounds are far above the practical benchmark, so they support the conclusion that these associations are not driven by weak-Cas9 screens in this dataset. Again, because these are discovery subsets in the same data and validation capacity is exhausted, they should be interpreted as technical robustness, not independent replication.\n\nH193 is the only new result I leave unresolved. In the KY-library subset, CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 dependency has a signed estimate of +0.116, but the lower bound is +0.047 and the exposed CDKN2A-loss cell has n=86. This is directionally consistent with the accepted main CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 association, but the interval does not robustly support a scientifically meaningful subset-specific effect above the practical benchmark. I therefore assess H193 as unresolved and deferred. The overall main association remains accepted based on earlier discovery and validation evidence, but library-specific attenuation within KY remains uncertain.\n\nH170 remains unresolved but deferred. Its extreme low-IFN estimate was positive but weak relative to the practical benchmark. H182 resolves the broader low-IFN question, so H170 should not drive further investigation unless a larger or more precise extreme-low-IFN analysis becomes available. Multiplicity and overlapping strata were considered; the accepted effects are large and interval-supported, so multiplicity is not a major concern here. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations, without causal or external-generalizability claims.",
