ted; high-screen-quality IFN interaction H224 remains unresolved. CDKN2A-loss dependency_MDM2 context tests do not generally certify modification; quality and IFN interactions are rejected or unresolved. Important negative findings: simple driver concordance is absent for KRAS/dependency_KRAS, BRAF V600E/dependency_BRAF, EGFR amplification/dependency_EGFR, ERBB2 amplification/dependency_ERBB2, and MYC amplification is largely null except a subbenchmark positive dependency_WRN effect. TP53 loss alone is not a practical predictor of MDM2, STAT3, WRN, or RPL11 dependency; PTEN loss is specific to STAT3 among tested outcomes; research markers D/E/F and many constructed signatures are null; H226 rejects high-screen amplification for PTEN-STAT3; H236 and H237 reject screen-quality and Cas9 modification for CDKN2A-MDM2; H234 rejects NF1 loss for STAT3; and many interaction tests are imprecise. Remaining uncertainty centers on whether accepted main effects are practical within specific technical or co-alteration strata, especially PTEN-loss STAT3 under high-screen-quality high IFN, CDKN2A-loss MDM2 in MTAP co-deletion, and small subset intervals. The most informative next same-dataset analyses are direct effect estimates rather than more overlapping interactions: direct PTEN-loss STAT3 in high-screen plus high-IFN and low-screen plus high-IFN strata, and direct CDKN2A-loss MDM2 in MTAP-loss and MTAP-intact strata. These are registered; I will accept them only if signed intervals clearly exceed the 0.1 benchmark, otherwise I will conclude robust main associations with limited or non-certified context modification.",
